The Engineering Design Process maps directly to the GitHub Workflow because both follow structured steps to build a final product. First, the team defines the problem and looks at the master project goals, which acts like the main branch holding the official version of the project. Next, each engineer takes on a specific component to design on their own blueprint sheet, which is just like creating a branch to work safely without altering the main plan. As the engineer sketches parts, runs tests, and adjusts dimensions, they make commits by saving small progress steps along the way. Once a part design is complete, the engineer presents the blueprint to the rest of the team to ask for feedback, which mirrors opening a pull request. The team then inspects the design, checks calculations, and suggests improvements, representing the review and discuss stage. Finally, after the team approves the blueprint, the new component gets built and added to the complete machine, which acts as the merge step by combining individual work into the final finished product.
